SAGA-Build addon: clutch-signals
=================================

O que é: o bridge compartilhado e neutro entre o ADIOS I/O scheduler
(escritor de io_pressure/topapp_active) e a Cerebral Clutch (CC) CPU
governor (leitor), mais o canal memory_pressure (dormente, ver
changelog abaixo).

Ordem de aplicação: aplicar ANTES de adios-clutch e de cc-governor. Se
qualquer um dos dois for aplicado sem este addon, o #include
<linux/clutch.h> falha -- comportamento esperado, pois clutch-signals é
dependência explícita de ambos.

O que ele fornece:
  - include/linux/clutch.h (declarações + stubs no-op quando
    CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS=n)
  - kernel/sched/clutch.c (storage: 3 atomic_t -- io_pressure,
    topapp_active, mem_pressure)
  - CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS em block/Kconfig.iosched (logo após o help de
    MQ_IOSCHED_KYBER, antes de IOSCHED_BFQ -- âncora que
    ADIOS_Clutch-Android-14-6.1.patch espera para o seu próprio bloco
    MQ_IOSCHED_*)
  - obj-$(CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS) += clutch.o em kernel/sched/Makefile

O que ele NÃO faz: não escreve nem lê nada por conta própria. Todos os
call sites (report/get) vivem nos consumidores (ADIOS, CC, subsistema
de memória).

-------------------------------------------------------------------------
Changelog desta revisão (A8 -- reversão da remoção de memory_pressure):

Uma rodada anterior removeu clutch_report_memory_pressure() /
clutch_get_memory_pressure() por serem dead code (zero writers, zero
readers -- o CC lê PGSCAN_DIRECT direto via all_vm_events(), nunca
passou pelo bridge). Decisão revisada nesta rodada: MANTER, revertendo
essa remoção. Motivo:

  - O canal não custa nada em runtime (dois atomic_t globais a mais,
    sem leitor nem escritor ativo).
  - PGSCAN_DIRECT cobre só reclaim direto; o trabalho do kswapd e do
    kcompressd -- que fazem a maior parte da compressão do ZRAM -- não
    aparece ali.
  - A frente ZRAM está ativa (integração recente de PGSCAN_DIRECT do
    lado CC), e esse canal é o lugar natural para receber o sinal mais
    completo quando/se um writer for implementado.

As duas funções voltaram exatamente como estavam antes da remoção:
clutch_report_memory_pressure(u32 pct_0_100) / clutch_get_memory_pressure()
em include/linux/clutch.h (declaração real sob CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS,
stub no-op no #else) e kernel/sched/clutch.c (atomic_t
clutch_mem_pressure, duas funções EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL, clamp 0..100).
ADIOS continua sem nenhuma mudança -- ele só inclui o header.

Se/quando um writer real for implementado (hook no subsistema de
memória), implementar no MESMO patch que o liga ao bridge -- não
especulativamente, como aconteceu aqui.

(A7 -- bridge "último a reportar vence": já documentado no comentário
de update_timer_callback() dentro de ADIOS_Clutch-Android-14-6.1.patch,
nenhuma mudança necessária nesta rodada.)

Verificação: patch -p1 --fuzz=0 aplica limpo contra clone fresco de
github.com/Andrews571/SAGA-Kernel-6.1 @ android14-6.1-live-staging. Os
4 arquivos tocados (include/linux/clutch.h, kernel/sched/clutch.c,
kernel/sched/Makefile, block/Kconfig.iosched) aplicam com zero
rejects, exit code 0. Em sequência com ADIOS_Clutch-Android-14-6.1.patch
também: exit 0, zero rejects.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
diff --git a/block/Kconfig.iosched b/block/Kconfig.iosched
index 615516146..120fd3e73 100644
--- a/block/Kconfig.iosched
+++ b/block/Kconfig.iosched
@@ -16,6 +16,25 @@ config MQ_IOSCHED_KYBER
 	  synchronous writes, it will self-tune queue depths to achieve that
 	  goal.
 
+config CLUTCH_SIGNALS
+	bool "Cross-subsystem Clutch signals (ADIOS <-> CC governor)"
+	default y if MQ_IOSCHED_ADIOS && CPU_FREQ_GOV_CC
+	default n
+	help
+	  Lightweight cross-subsystem signals between the ADIOS I/O
+	  scheduler and the Cerebral Clutch CPU governor. When enabled,
+	  ADIOS reports I/O pressure and top-app activity (~100ms cadence)
+	  and the CC governor reads them to gate iowait boost and, if
+	  configured, confirm wake-edges early. When disabled, all calls
+	  compile to no-ops and neither consumer needs any change.
+
+	  Self-sufficient as an addon: define this symbol here, provide
+	  include/linux/clutch.h and kernel/sched/clutch.c, and both the
+	  ADIOS and CC patches pick it up automatically. Either consumer
+	  can be absent -- the "default y if" expression evaluates to
+	  false when either symbol is undefined, so this addon is inert
+	  in a build that has only one of them.
+
 config IOSCHED_BFQ
 	tristate "BFQ I/O scheduler"
 	select BLK_ICQ
diff --git a/include/linux/clutch.h b/include/linux/clutch.h
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..7b7ef5f95
--- /dev/null
+++ b/include/linux/clutch.h
@@ -0,0 +1,69 @@
+/* SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0 */
+#ifndef _LINUX_CLUTCH_H
+#define _LINUX_CLUTCH_H
+
+/*
+ * Clutch bridge -- shared, neutral interface between the ADIOS I/O
+ * scheduler (writer: clutch_report_io_pressure()) and the CC_GOVERNOR
+ * cpufreq governor (reader: clutch_get_io_pressure(),
+ * clutch_get_topapp_active()). Storage lives in kernel/sched/clutch.c,
+ * a location neither block/ (ADIOS) nor drivers/cpufreq/ (CC) owns, so
+ * either side can #include this header without a dependency on the
+ * other's source tree.
+ *
+ * This header is written to compile and link safely no matter which of
+ * the two consumers (ADIOS, CC_GOVERNOR) are applied, in any
+ * combination, including none:
+ *
+ *   - CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS undefined (feature is off): every function
+ *     below is a static inline stub that touches nothing and returns a
+ *     fixed, safe value (0 / false). No real functions exist to link
+ *     against, and none are needed -- callers on both sides always
+ *     compile.
+ *   - CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS=y (this addon is applied): the real,
+ *     atomic-backed implementation is used instead, declared (not
+ *     defined) here and exported via EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL from clutch.c.
+ *
+ * Neither consumer needs to wrap call sites in #ifdef CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS
+ * themselves -- that's handled once, here.
+ *
+ * Memory pressure (clutch_report_memory_pressure() /
+ * clutch_get_memory_pressure()) is reported by the memory subsystem
+ * itself (kswapd, kcompressd, direct reclaim, ...), not by ADIOS --
+ * ADIOS never calls the report function, it only ever #includes this
+ * header. Kept even though no writer is implemented yet (decision:
+ * KEEP, not dead-code-remove): the CC side today only sees direct
+ * reclaim via PGSCAN_DIRECT through all_vm_events(), a separate path
+ * that bypasses this bridge entirely -- but kswapd/kcompressd do most
+ * of the actual ZRAM compression work and never show up in
+ * PGSCAN_DIRECT. The cost of keeping an unused atomic_t is zero at
+ * runtime, and if the ZRAM-pressure work continues (it's active, see
+ * the recent PGSCAN_DIRECT integration on the CC side), this is the
+ * natural channel for the more complete signal once a writer is
+ * implemented -- together, in the same patch as the writer, not ahead
+ * of it.
+ */
+
+#include <linux/types.h>
+
+#ifdef CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS
+
+void clutch_report_io_pressure(u32 pct_0_100, bool topapp_active);
+u32 clutch_get_io_pressure(void);
+bool clutch_get_topapp_active(void);
+
+void clutch_report_memory_pressure(u32 pct_0_100);
+u32 clutch_get_memory_pressure(void);
+
+#else
+
+static inline void clutch_report_io_pressure(u32 pct_0_100, bool topapp_active) {}
+static inline u32 clutch_get_io_pressure(void) { return 0; }
+static inline bool clutch_get_topapp_active(void) { return false; }
+
+static inline void clutch_report_memory_pressure(u32 pct_0_100) {}
+static inline u32 clutch_get_memory_pressure(void) { return 0; }
+
+#endif /* CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS */
+
+#endif /* _LINUX_CLUTCH_H */
diff --git a/kernel/sched/Makefile b/kernel/sched/Makefile
index ff1738cec..4a47e3de4 100644
--- a/kernel/sched/Makefile
+++ b/kernel/sched/Makefile
@@ -33,3 +33,8 @@ obj-y += fair.o
 obj-y += build_policy.o
 obj-y += build_utility.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_ANDROID_VENDOR_HOOKS) += vendor_hooks.o
+
+# Clutch bridge (SAGA) -- neutral storage shared between the ADIOS I/O
+# scheduler (writer) and CC_GOVERNOR (reader), see include/linux/clutch.h.
+# Provided by the clutch-signals addon; both consumers depend on it.
+obj-$(CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS) += clutch.o
diff --git a/kernel/sched/clutch.c b/kernel/sched/clutch.c
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..9ff6ecf05
--- /dev/null
+++ b/kernel/sched/clutch.c
@@ -0,0 +1,66 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+/*
+ * Clutch bridge storage -- see include/linux/clutch.h for the full
+ * picture. This file only exists in the build when CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS
+ * is set (see the obj-$(CONFIG_CLUTCH_SIGNALS) rule in
+ * kernel/sched/Makefile), so nothing here needs its own #ifdef guard.
+ *
+ * Plain atomic_t, not a spinlock-protected struct: all three fields
+ * are independent, single-word values with no ordering requirement
+ * between them (a reader seeing an io_pressure update slightly before
+ * or after a topapp_active update from the same ADIOS report is fine
+ * -- neither consumer treats them as a single atomic snapshot).
+ * atomic_read() is non-blocking and safe from any context, including
+ * under a raw spinlock (CC_GOVERNOR's shared-policy path takes one
+ * around exactly this kind of read).
+ */
+#include <linux/clutch.h>
+#include <linux/atomic.h>
+#include <linux/export.h>
+
+static atomic_t clutch_io_pressure = ATOMIC_INIT(0);
+static atomic_t clutch_topapp_active = ATOMIC_INIT(0);
+static atomic_t clutch_mem_pressure = ATOMIC_INIT(0);
+
+void clutch_report_io_pressure(u32 pct_0_100, bool topapp_active)
+{
+	if (pct_0_100 > 100)
+		pct_0_100 = 100;
+	atomic_set(&clutch_io_pressure, (int)pct_0_100);
+	atomic_set(&clutch_topapp_active, topapp_active ? 1 : 0);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(clutch_report_io_pressure);
+
+u32 clutch_get_io_pressure(void)
+{
+	return (u32)atomic_read(&clutch_io_pressure);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(clutch_get_io_pressure);
+
+bool clutch_get_topapp_active(void)
+{
+	return atomic_read(&clutch_topapp_active) != 0;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(clutch_get_topapp_active);
+
+/*
+ * Memory pressure (ZRAM reclaim etc.) is reported by the memory
+ * subsystem itself (kswapd, kcompressd, direct reclaim, ...), not by
+ * ADIOS -- ADIOS only ever #includes this header, it never calls
+ * clutch_report_memory_pressure(). No writer is implemented yet
+ * (decision: KEEP as dormant channel, not dead-code-remove) -- see
+ * the comment in include/linux/clutch.h for why.
+ */
+void clutch_report_memory_pressure(u32 pct_0_100)
+{
+	if (pct_0_100 > 100)
+		pct_0_100 = 100;
+	atomic_set(&clutch_mem_pressure, (int)pct_0_100);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(clutch_report_memory_pressure);
+
+u32 clutch_get_memory_pressure(void)
+{
+	return (u32)atomic_read(&clutch_mem_pressure);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(clutch_get_memory_pressure);
